{
  "id": "reverse-bits",
  "questionId": "reverse-bits",
  "slug": "reverse-bits",
  "title": "Reverse Bits",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you need to move the lowest bit of n to become the highest bit of the result, the second-lowest to become the second-highest, and so on - can you do this in a single pass?",
    "at each of the 32 steps, grab n's current lowest bit with n & 1, then remove it from n by shifting n right by 1",
    "build up the result by shifting it left by 1 first (to make room), then OR-ing in the bit you just grabbed from n",
    "after 32 iterations of 'shift result left, OR in n's lowest bit, shift n right', result holds n's bits fully reversed - remember to treat both n and result as unsigned 32-bit values so the sign bit doesn't cause issues in languages with signed integers"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to treat the value as unsigned, causing incorrect results in languages (like Java or C++) where plain int is signed and the high bit means negative",
    "looping fewer or more than exactly 32 times",
    "shifting result before OR-ing in the new bit versus after, and getting the bit order backwards"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an integer `n` representing the bit pattern of a 32-bit\nunsigned integer.\n\n**Return the integer whose 32-bit pattern is `n`'s bits reversed (the most significant bit becomes the least significant bit, and so on).**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 2^32 - 1",
      "n is given as a 32-bit unsigned integer",
      "0 <= result <= 2^32 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (reverses to 0)",
      "n has only its lowest bit set (reverses to only its highest bit set)",
      "n uses the full 32-bit range, producing a result larger than 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
